  ],
  "text": "A rocket is a fuel tank with an engine bolted on. The payload is a few percent of the whole thing. To stay in orbit you need to be moving sideways at about twenty eight thousand kilometres an hour. That is not about height. It is about speed. Height is the easy half. And here is the trap. Fuel has weight, so you need fuel just to lift the fuel. Add more and you have added more mass to lift, which needs more fuel again. It does not add up. It compounds, and it compounds against you. Which is why almost the whole rocket is tank, and the useful cargo is a sliver at the top. The workaround is to stop carrying what you have already emptied. Drop the empty tank in flight and everything above it becomes far easier to push. Space is expensive because of arithmetic. It was a mass problem long before it was a money problem. One of these every day. Follow so you do not miss tomorrow.",
  "voice": "802e3bc2b27e49c2995d23ef70e6ac89",
  "model": "s2.1-pro-free"
}
